\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-x+2{y}^{2} \\\\ -4x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+2{y}^{2})=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+2{y}^{2})=4y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+{x}^{2})=-4+2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4y \\\\ -4+2x & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4(0) \\\\ -4+2(0) & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444534 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1391978c-3f23-5276-a4d7-85ffc5372cc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444534 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{x} & -2y \\\\ -2x & -{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444535 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)-2x(t)^{3}+y(t)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)-3y(t)^{3}+x(t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3df14087-9cbc-58b7-a996-90852883ca3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444535 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-4x-2{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} \\\\ -3y-3{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x-2{x}^{3}+{y}^{2})=-4-6{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x-2{x}^{3}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y-3{y}^{3}+{x}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y-3{y}^{3}+{x}^{2})=-3-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-4-6{x}^{2} & 2y \\\\ 2x & -3-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-4-6(0)^{2} & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -3-9(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-3,\\text{ }-4.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real and negative eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-366655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+3y(t)+3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+2{y}^{2}(t)-y(t)+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4189cf97-4019-5e9f-9f66-1a0dc740de8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444536 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+3y+3 \\\\ -4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2)-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4(-1)-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444537 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+x(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(2,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45a54fce-a708-5a31-9c28-340df3e868af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444537 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-5 \\\\ {y}^{2}+x-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-5)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-5)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+x-3)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(2,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(2) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444538 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t)+(x(t)-2)^{3}+8 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-8]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c33868d-8235-5c25-b35a-535f5738b142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444538 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y+(x-2)^{3}+8 \\\\ -2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+(x-2)^{3}+8)=3(x-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8))=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}3(x-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-8]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}3((2)-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8+8)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444539 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x(t))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y(t)^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73b75bfd-1216-5131-9b02-68e4ee57dee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444539 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & \\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+(0)^{2}} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=3,\\text{ }5.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues,, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444540 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}+y-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}={[0,\\text{ }1]}^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74a51538-7992-5c00-9322-0a9614a20c67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444540 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-1)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-1)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=\\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -\\frac{1}{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{6}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba7562c9-dc1e-5d77-b34b-c5d59c44924f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444541 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-7x \\\\ {x}^{2}+6y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-7x)=-7,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-7x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+6y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+6y)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2y \\\\ 2x & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-4x(t)+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8e22bce-e115-5d92-9ec4-991287c11960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444542 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+y-4x+4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=2x+3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=-2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & -2y+2 \\\\ 2x-4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)+3 & -2(-1)+2 \\\\ 2(1)-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444543 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)+x(t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9194ecf-6556-5bbf-bbe7-0cf26c275fb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444543 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-3x+{y}^{2} \\\\ -2y+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3x+{y}^{2})=-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+{x}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+{x}^{2})=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2,\\text{ }-3.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real and negative eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
